.he "ls (1)                       8/12/26                       ls (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
ls
- list the contents of a directory
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
ls [ -acdfgilrstu ] [ name ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
For each directory named, ls lists what is in it; for each file, ls
lists the file.  With no name at all it lists the current directory.
The listing is sorted by name unless an option says otherwise.
.sp
By default the names are printed several to a line, as many as fit in
eighty columns.  Any option that has something to say about each file
- -l, -s or -i - turns that off and prints one name to a line.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-l	Long: mode, links, owner, size and time as well as the name.

.ti -10
-s	Give the size of each file in blocks.

.ti -10
-i	Give the i-number of each file.

.ti -10
-a	List entries beginning with a period, which are otherwise left out.

.ti -10
-d	List a directory itself rather than its contents.

.ti -10
-t	Sort by time rather than by name, most recent first.

.ti -10
-u	Use the time of last use rather than last change.

.ti -10
-c	Use the time the inode was last changed.

.ti -10
-r	Reverse whichever order is in force.

.ti -10
-g	In a long listing, give the group rather than the owner.

.ti -10
-f	Take each argument to be a directory and read it without sorting or stat'ing, which is quick and shows the order the entries are really in.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The mode column writes an l rather than a w for the write bit when the
file is marked for exclusive write, which is a micronix bit that v7
does not have.
.sp
A file's size is kept in three bytes here, a high byte and a low word,
so a long listing can report a size up to sixteen megabytes.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
pwd (1), rm (1)
